.TH AS 1 "RAINBOW/VENIX Only"
.SH NAME
as \- assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX
.br
.B as
[
.B \-
] [
.B \-m
] [
.B \-o
objfile ] file ...
.PP
.B RAINBOW/VENIX
.br
.B as
file[.s]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B as
is the VENIX assembler.
The primary purpose of this assembler is to
be used by the C or Fortran compilers; it is not normally executed
directly by the user.
Assembler listing of C or Fortran may be generated
by using the
.B \-S
flag with
.B cc
or
.BR f77 .
.TP
.B \-
All undefined symbols are treated as global.
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only).
.TP
.B \-m
``Code-mapping'' (memory overlaying) is to be used.
This alters the assembly output format slightly.
This flag may in fact be used at all times under VENIX,
whether or not code-mapping is actually invoked at load-time.
However, it will cause incompatibilities with other PDP-11 UNIX assemblers.
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only).
.TP
.B \-o
The object code output will be placed in the following file,
instead of
.BR a.out .
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only).
In RAINBOW/VENIX, the output file name is taken from the
source file (only one allowed), with
.B `.o'
replacing the optional
.B `.s'
in the name.
.PP
.B NOTES FOR RAINBOW/VENIX
.br
Compiler additional assembler documentation is not provided for
RAINBOW/VENIX.
The following brief notes are provided for users
who insist in writing assembly code; further details must be gleaned from
assembler listings produced by the C compiler.
.PP
The basic RAINBOW/VENIX assembler syntax is:
.PP
.RS
.RS
label:  op[b] [dst/src][,src] | comments
.RE
.RE
.PP
where
.I dst/src
take the form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 1.2i
reg:	ax,bx,cx,dx,bp,si,di,sp and also h,l for x
constant:	* for byte or # for word with normal C constants
memory:	label
index:	* number (reg) or # number (reg)
indirect:	(reg) except no (bp)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Several relevant pseudo ops:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 1.2i 2.8i
\&.text		|text or code space
\&.data		|data space
\&.comm	name, size	|BSS space
\&.globl	name
\&.word	constant [, ...]
\&.byte	constant [, ...]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The input and output instructions are:
.RS
.TP 1.8i
IN
reads a byte from port address specified in
DX register into AL register.
.TP
IN port address
reads a byte from specified port address into
AL register.
.TP
OUT
writes a byte from the AL register to the
port address specified into the DX register.
.TP
OUT port address
writes a byte from the specified port address
into the DX register.
.RE
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.2i
file.s	assembler source file
file.o	object (RAINBOW/VENIX)
a.out	object (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cc(1), ld(1), nm(1), adb(1), a.out(4)
.br
``VENIX Assembler Reference Manual'' in the
.I "Programming Guide"
.SH "EXAMPLES (RAINBOW/VENIX only)"
An example of a RAINBOW/VENIX assembler subroutine callable from C.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta .8i 2.4i
|
| add (a,b)	/* return a + b */
|
\&.text
\&.globl _add
_add:	push bp	|save old frame pointer
	mov bp,sp	|get new pointer
	mov ax,*4(bp)	|get first argument
	add ax,*6(bp)	|add second to it
	pop bp	|restore old frame pointer
	ret	|return value in ax
.fi
.RE
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)"
When an input file cannot be read, its name followed by a question mark
is typed and assembly ceases.
When syntactic or semantic errors occur, a
single-character diagnostic is typed out together with the line number and
the file name in which it occurred.
Errors in pass 1 cause cancellation of pass 2.
The possible errors are:
.PP
.nf
.ta .5i
)	Parentheses error
]	Parentheses error
<	String not terminated properly
*	Indirection used illegally
\&.	Illegal assignment to `.'
a	Error in address
b	Branch instruction is odd or too remote
e	Error in expression
f	Error in local (`f' or `b') type symbol
g	Garbage (unknown) character
i	End of file inside an if
m	Multiply defined symbol as label
o	Word quantity assembled at odd address
p	`.' different in pass 1 and 2
r	Relocation error
u	Undefined symbol
x	Syntax error
.fi
.SH BUGS
The RAINBOW/VENIX assembler does not conform to the Intel assembler specification.
.sp
